# Template. build.sh replaces the placeholder line below with the minted
# certificate and writes the result to $WORK/policy.yaml. The same file is loaded by the
# bootstrap attach and compiled into bpfjcmd, so both halves agree by
# construction.
#
# Two roles, and the shape of each is load-bearing.
#
# `floor` is the base role, so every process on the host is in it. `any` leaves
# execs and other unspecified operations open -- a base role with
# `enforce-binary-certs` would demand a signature from every binary on the
# host, which is not what this example is about.
#
# `bpf-pod` narrows BPF from that open baseline to objects from the caller's
# own pod.
#
# `untracked-bpf` is what keeps that from meaning the opposite. Configuring a
# role for bpf is also what makes it take ownership of what it creates, and a
# base role is held by every process on the host -- so without this `floor`
# would come to own every BPF object on the machine, the restriction would be
# to the whole host, and `bpfjailer` could not reach the jailer's own maps.
#
# `bpfjailer` is the role a helper enters through `bpfjctl enroll`.
#
#   override-stacked  this role answers on its own. A task that execs the
#                     signed binary holds [floor, bpfjailer]; every enforcer
#                     walks that newest first and stops here, so `floor` gets
#                     no say over what the upgrade binary may do.
#   enforce-binary-certs
#                     no binary may take this role without a signature from
#                     the key below, over its fs-verity digest.
#   min-seq:          the lowest signed sequence number the role still accepts.
#                     A valid signature below it is refused, so a binary can be
#                     retired without changing the key that signed it.
#   bpf-any:          may call bpf(2) and reach the unowned bootstrap maps as
#                     well as the maps its replacements create.
#   keyring-own:      may rewrite its own role's keyring,
#                     which is what a replace does when it reloads the
#                     fs-verity enforcer, and no other role's.
base-role: floor
certs:
  signer: |
@CERT@
roles:
  floor:
    any: true
    bpf-pod: true
    untracked-bpf: true
    keyring-own: true
  bpfjailer:
    any: true
    override-stacked: true
    enforce-binary-certs:
      - signer
    min-seq: @CURRENT_SEQ@
    bpf-any: true
    keyring-own: true
